PLACES=[
N('academy-of-music-philadelphia','Academy of Music','Philadelphia','pae','civic','standing',39.94802,-75.16520,'exact','view','walk','','1855–1857',
  'A concert hall and opera house at Broad and Locust Streets, built in 1855–57 to a competition design by Napoleon LeBrun and Gustavus Runge, and called the oldest opera house in the United States still used for its first purpose. It opened with a ball in January 1857 and was home to the Philadelphia Orchestra from 1900 until 2001; the Philadelphia Ballet and Opera Philadelphia still perform there. A National Historic Landmark since 1962.',
  'A plain round-arched exterior of brick and brownstone, kept plain so the money could go into the auditorium; extensive renovations from 1994 kept the hall as built. The auditorium is seen only with a ticket to a performance; interior not checked.',
  '','Owned by the Philadelphia Orchestra in partnership with Ensemble Arts; 240 South Broad Street. Seen from the street at any time; inside only at performances. '+CC,
  [W('Academy of Music (Philadelphia)'),['Academy of Music','https://www.academyofmusic.org/']],brick='?'),
N('bellevue-stratford-hotel-philadelphia','Bellevue-Stratford Hotel (The Bellevue)','Philadelphia','pae','hotel','standing',39.94909,-75.16487,'exact','open','walk','','1902–1904, enlarged 1911–1912',
  'A French Renaissance hotel at Broad and Walnut Streets, opened in 1904 and carried to nineteen storeys and about 1,090 rooms by 1912, when the domed function rooms on the top floor were added. In 1976 an outbreak of a pneumonia-like illness among American Legion delegates, later traced to its air conditioning, gave Legionnaires\' disease its name; the original hotel closed for good in 1986. Since 1988 it has been a mixed building called The Bellevue, with a hotel on its upper floors.',
  'A renovation finished in 2024 brought the ground-floor lobby back to its 1904 plan, with the portico and the elliptical staircase put back. A working hotel, open to guests and diners; the upper ballrooms not checked.',
  '','A hotel run by Hyatt, 200 South Broad Street. '+CC,
  [W('The Bellevue-Stratford Hotel')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('land-title-building-philadelphia','Land Title Building','Philadelphia','pae','civic','standing',39.95058,-75.16435,'exact','view','walk','','1897–1902',
  'An early skyscraper of two towers joined at the ground floor, built for the Land Title and Trust Company, said to be the oldest title insurance company in the world. Daniel Burnham of Chicago designed the fifteen-storey northern tower of 1898; the 22-storey southern tower of 1902 is by Burnham with Horace Trumbauer, on the site of the old Lafayette Hotel. On the National Register since 1978.',
  'Two neoclassical office towers on South Broad Street at Chestnut, one block south of City Hall. A state historical marker outside remembers Anne Brancato Wood, a legislator whose offices were here. Interior not checked.',
  '','Private office building, 100 South Broad Street; seen from the sidewalk. '+CC,
  [W('Land Title Building')],brick='?'),
N('union-league-of-philadelphia','Union League of Philadelphia','Philadelphia','pae','civic','standing',39.94998,-75.16462,'exact','view','walk','','1865, enlarged 1910–1911',
  'A club founded in November 1862 to back the Union and President Lincoln. Its Second Empire clubhouse on Broad Street, designed by John Fraser, was finished in May 1865; Beaux-Arts additions of 1910 and 1911 carried it through to fill the whole block. On the National Register since 1979.',
  'A brick and brownstone Second Empire front on Broad Street, and the later Beaux-Arts wing behind. A private members\' club; interior not checked.',
  '','Private club, 140 South Broad Street at Sansom Street; look from the sidewalk. '+CC,
  [W('Union League of Philadelphia')],brick='?'),
N('philadelphia-city-hall','Philadelphia City Hall','Philadelphia','pae','civic','standing',39.95239,-75.16360,'exact','open','walk','','1871–1901',
  'The seat of the city government and of city courts, built in the Second Empire style from 1871 to 1901 to designs by John McArthur Jr. and Thomas Ustick Walter, on Centre Square, one of William Penn\'s five original squares. With the statue of Penn on top, its 548-foot tower made it the tallest habitable building in the world from 1894 to 1908, and by an unwritten rule nothing in the city rose higher until One Liberty Place in 1986. Called the largest free-standing masonry building in the world; a National Historic Landmark since 1976.',
  'Brick walls faced with white marble and limestone, sculpture by Alexander Milne Calder, and the statue of William Penn on the tower. The inside is seen on the guided building tour, and the tower has its own tour (search results in November 2026 quoted a January 2025 notice that the tower was shut for maintenance).',
  '','City of Philadelphia, Penn Square at Broad and Market Streets. Tours leave from the City Hall Visitor Center, Room 121 in the east portal, and are sold by the visitor center; advance booking is advised. The hours are as search results quoted the visitor center\'s pages, which could not be opened when this was written. '+CC,
  [W('Philadelphia City Hall'),['City Hall Visitor Center: building tour','https://www.phlvisitorcenter.com/city-hall-building-tour']],brick='no',
  hours='Building tour weekdays at 10 a.m., noon and 2 p.m. (as quoted from the visitor center)'),
N('us-custom-house-philadelphia','United States Custom House','Philadelphia','pae','civic','standing',39.94798,-75.14477,'exact','view','walk','','1932–1934',
  'A seventeen-storey federal building of 1932–34 in the Art Deco manner, by the Philadelphia firm Ritter & Shay, filling the block between Second, Chestnut and Sansom Streets in the oldest part of the city. It replaced the Second Bank of the United States, the customs service\'s home since 1845, and was built as a Depression relief project for over $3.5 million. Rehabilitated in 1991–93 and again in 2010–13; on the National Register since 2011.',
  'A tall tower beside Independence National Historical Park, with Art Deco decoration by a local artist. Federal offices: what the public may see inside was not checked.',
  '','Federal office building (U.S. General Services Administration), 200 Chestnut Street; look from the street. '+CC,
  [W('United States Custom House (Philadelphia)')],brick='?'),
N('masonic-temple-philadelphia','Masonic Temple','Philadelphia','pae','civic','standing',39.95362,-75.16266,'exact','open','walk','','1868–1873, interior to 1887',
  'Headquarters of the Grand Lodge of Pennsylvania, facing City Hall across Broad Street. James H. Windrim, aged 27, won the design competition with a Norman Revival scheme; the ten-ton granite cornerstone was laid on June 24, 1868 with the gavel George Washington used at the Capitol in 1793, and the building was dedicated in September 1873. George Herzog decorated the interior from 1887. On the National Register since 1971.',
  'Granite fronts with a Norman portal on Broad Street. Inside are seven lodge rooms, still used for meetings, and the Masonic Library and Museum. Visitors see them only on a guided tour: the Temple\'s page says no one may walk the building alone.',
  '','Grand Lodge of Pennsylvania, 1 North Broad Street. Guided tours only; days, times and closures are on the Temple\'s own pages, and its hours page could not be opened when this was written. '+CC,
  [W('Masonic Temple (Philadelphia, Pennsylvania)'),['Masonic Temple, Library and Museum: plan your visit','https://pamasonictemple.org/plan-your-visit']],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('pennsylvania-academy-fine-arts-furness-building','Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts (Furness-Hewitt Building)','Philadelphia','pae','civic','standing',39.95524,-75.16367,'exact','open','walk','','1871–1876',
  'The oldest art museum and art school in the United States, founded in 1805. Its third home, by Frank Furness and George Hewitt, was begun in 1871 and opened for the Centennial of 1876; it mixes Second Empire, Renaissance and Gothic ideas and is counted among Furness\'s best works. A renovation finished in 2019 kept its historic detail. A National Historic Landmark.',
  'The museum is open to visitors; its collections are American paintings, sculpture and works on paper of the 19th and 20th centuries. Interior detail not checked.',
  '','Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts, 118–128 North Broad Street at Cherry Street; admission charged. Hours from the Academy\'s visit page. '+CC,
  [W('Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts'),['PAFA: visit','https://www.pafa.org/visit']],brick='?',
  hours='Thursday to Monday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Fridays to 8 p.m.; closed Tuesday and Wednesday'),
N('reading-terminal-headhouse-philadelphia','Reading Terminal and Reading Terminal Market','Philadelphia','pae','rail','standing',39.95215,-75.15926,'exact','open','walk','stations','1891–1893',
  'The Philadelphia and Reading Railway\'s city station and headquarters at 12th and Market Streets, opened on January 29, 1893. The headhouse is by Francis H. Kimball and the great train shed by Wilson Brothers; to build it the railroad bought out the open-air market on the site and gave it a new hall under the tracks, the Reading Terminal Market, still open. In the 1990s the headhouse became the main entrance of the Pennsylvania Convention Center and the train shed its Grand Hall.',
  'An Italian Renaissance headhouse with brick bearing walls, cast-iron columns and ornamental plaster and marble inside, and the market beneath the old train shed on Filbert Street.',
  '','Headhouse: part of the Pennsylvania Convention Center, 1115 Market Street, with shops and restaurants on its street and concourse levels. The market, under the train shed at 12th and Arch Streets, gives its hours on its own page and says it closes on some holidays. SEPTA stops at Jefferson Station and 11th Street. '+CC,
  [W('Reading Terminal'),['Reading Terminal Market','https://readingterminalmarket.org/']],brick='?',
  hours='Market daily 8 a.m. to 6 p.m.'),
N('wanamaker-building-philadelphia','Wanamaker Building','Philadelphia','pae','civic','standing',39.95152,-75.16212,'exact','view','walk','','1902–1911',
  'John Wanamaker\'s flagship department store, designed by Daniel Burnham and opened in 1911 on the site of Wanamaker\'s first store of 1876. Its marble-clad Grand Court holds the Wanamaker Organ, first built for the St. Louis World\'s Fair and enlarged by the store until it had some 28,750 pipes, the largest working pipe organ in the world. The building passed to Hecht\'s, Strawbridge\'s, Lord & Taylor and Macy\'s; Macy\'s closed the store on March 23, 2025, and in October 2026 plans were announced for 622 apartments above and a food hall in the Grand Court.',
  'A Florentine palace of granite filling the block at 13th and Market Streets, across from City Hall. With the store closed, what the public may see of the Grand Court and the organ was not checked.',
  '','Private, 1300 Market Street; seen from the street while its future use is settled. '+CC,
  [W('John Wanamaker Store')],brick='no'),
N('witherspoon-building-philadelphia','Witherspoon Building','Philadelphia','pae','civic','standing',39.94972,-75.16317,'exact','view','walk','','1896',
  'An eleven-storey office building of 1896 by Joseph M. Huston, put up for the Presbyterian Board of Publications and Sabbath School Work and named for John Witherspoon, a president of Princeton. The Philadelphia College and Infirmary of Osteopathy began here in 1899. On the National Register since 1978.',
  'A steel-framed E-shaped block faced with brick and granite, with Corinthian and Ionic columns, church seals and medallions, and statues of six early Presbyterians (Makemie, Witherspoon, McMillan, Davies, Caldwell and Whitman), several of them by Alexander Stirling Calder. Interior not checked.',
  '','Private office building, 1319–1323 Walnut Street; look from the sidewalk. '+CC,
  [W('Witherspoon Building')],brick='?'),
N('cathedral-basilica-sts-peter-paul-philadelphia','Cathedral Basilica of Saints Peter and Paul','Philadelphia','pae','worship','standing',39.95725,-75.16884,'exact','open','walk','','1846–1864',
  'The cathedral of the Archdiocese of Philadelphia and the largest Catholic church in Pennsylvania, seating 2,000. Napoleon LeBrun designed it and work began in 1846; John Notman added the dome and the Palladian front after 1850, and Constantino Brumidi painted the interior. It was dedicated in 1864 and made a minor basilica in 1976. On the National Register since 1971.',
  'An Italian Renaissance church with a dome and a Palladian front, at 18th Street and the Benjamin Franklin Parkway. Brumidi\'s murals inside; open for Mass and to visitors.',
  '','Archdiocese of Philadelphia, 18th Street and the Benjamin Franklin Parkway (1723 Race Street). Mass times from the Archdiocese\'s parish page; the cathedral\'s own site, which gives its daily opening hours, could not be opened when this was written. '+CC,
  [W('Cathedral Basilica of Saints Peter and Paul (Philadelphia)'),['Archdiocese of Philadelphia: Cathedral Basilica parish page','https://archphila.org/parish/cathedral-basilica-of-ss-peter-paul-philadelphia/']],brick='no',
  hours='Mass Saturday 5:15 p.m.; Sunday 8, 9:30, 11 a.m., 12:30 (Spanish), 6:30 p.m.; weekdays 7:15 a.m., 12:05 p.m.'),
N('fairmount-water-works-philadelphia','Fairmount Water Works','Philadelphia','pae','water','standing',39.96619,-75.18353,'exact','open','walk','','1812–1909',
  'The city\'s second waterworks, designed by Frederick Graff and finished in 1815 below the reservoir on Faire Mount, where the Museum of Art now stands. A dam built across the Schuylkill in 1819–21 turned water wheels in a mill house from 1822, the first in the country to pump a city\'s water that way; turbines came later. It closed in 1909 and was later an aquarium, until 1962. A National Historic Landmark since 1976.',
  'Palladian-style mill houses and pavilions on the riverbank below the art museum, with the 1928 dam and Boathouse Row beside it. The Interpretive Center inside tells the story of the works and the watershed; admission is free.',
  '','City of Philadelphia (Philadelphia Water Department), 640 Waterworks Drive, off Kelly Drive. The riverside grounds and walks are open ground; the Interpretive Center\'s hours are from its own visit page. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Fairmount Water Works'),['Fairmount Water Works: visit','https://fairmountwaterworks.org/visit/']],brick='?',
  hours='Interpretive Center Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('baptist-temple-philadelphia','Baptist Temple (Temple Performing Arts Center)','Philadelphia','pae','worship','standing',39.98144,-75.15681,'exact','view','walk','','1891',
  'The church of Grace Baptist congregation under Russell H. Conwell, designed by Thomas Preston Lonsdale and opened in 1891. With up to 15,000 worshippers on a Sunday it was one of the first "institutional churches" in the country, and the congregation gave rise to Temple University. Temple bought the building in 1974, and after years of disuse renovated it as the Temple Performing Arts Center in 2010.',
  'A large church on North Broad Street on the Temple University campus; the auditorium keeps a stained glass window. Inside only at events; interior otherwise not checked.',
  '','Temple University, 1837 North Broad Street; look from the sidewalk, or attend an event. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Grace Baptist Church of Philadelphia')],brick='no'),
N('first-baptist-church-philadelphia-17th-sansom','First Baptist Church (17th and Sansom)','Philadelphia','pae','worship','standing',39.95049,-75.16863,'exact','view','walk','','1899–1900',
  'The congregation began in 1698 and is one of the oldest Baptist churches in the country; it moved from Second and Arch Streets to Broad and Arch in 1855, and built this church at 17th and Sansom Streets in 1899–1900 to designs by Edgar Viguers Seeler, mixing Byzantine and Romanesque Revival. The building was sold in 2014. Wikipedia says it went to Liberti Church; OpenStreetMap labels it Resurrection Philadelphia: who worships here now is not confirmed.',
  'A Byzantine-Romanesque church at the corner of 17th and Sansom Streets, a block from Rittenhouse Square. Interior not checked.',
  '','A church, 123 South 17th Street; look from the street. Services and visiting were not checked. '+CC,
  [W('First Baptist Church (Philadelphia)')],brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('metropolitan-opera-house-philadelphia','Metropolitan Opera House (The Met Philadelphia)','Philadelphia','pae','civic','standing',39.96983,-75.16035,'exact','view','walk','','1908',
  'Built in 1908 as Oscar Hammerstein I\'s Philadelphia Opera House, his ninth, and sold in 1910 to the Metropolitan Opera of New York, which renamed it and used it through the 1920s. It was later a cinema, a ballroom, a sports hall, a mechanics\' school and a church. Restored, it reopened on December 3, 2018 as a concert hall run by Live Nation.',
  'A large theatre on North Broad Street at Poplar Street. Inside only with a ticket to a show; interior not checked.',
  '','Concert venue, 858 North Broad Street; look from the street, or go to a show. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Metropolitan Opera House (Philadelphia)')],brick='?'),
N('college-hall-university-of-pennsylvania','College Hall, University of Pennsylvania','Philadelphia','pae','inst','standing',39.95150,-75.19376,'exact','view','walk','','1871–1873',
  'The oldest building on the University of Pennsylvania\'s West Philadelphia campus, designed by Thomas Webb Richards and finished in 1873, when the university moved out from Ninth Street in Center City. It holds admissions, the president\'s offices and the history department; the top floor was built for two student literary societies and is still home to the Philomathean Society, founded 1813. On the National Register since 1978.',
  'A hall built of green serpentine stone, which gives it its colour. A working university building: interior not checked.',
  '','University of Pennsylvania, 3450 Woodland Walk, on the campus in University City; look from the campus walks. Parking not checked.',
  [W('College Hall (University of Pennsylvania)')],brick='no'),
N('drake-hotel-philadelphia','Drake Hotel','Philadelphia','pae','hotel','standing',39.94702,-75.16722,'exact','view','walk','','1928–1929',
  'A 33-storey, 375-foot hotel tower on Spruce Street near Rittenhouse Square, built in 1928–29 by the Murphy, Quigley Company to designs by Ritter and Shay. It is Art Deco with Spanish Baroque terra cotta on the theme of Sir Francis Drake: dolphins, shells, ships and globes, and a terra cotta dome on top.',
  'Look up for the terra cotta ornament and the dome. Now apartments; interior not checked.',
  '','Private apartment building, 1512–1514 Spruce Street; look from the street. '+CC,
  [W('Drake Hotel (Philadelphia)')],brick='?'),
]
